\description{
The type vocabulary is expressed as the class hierarchy rather than as a
\code{type} string checked against a list of permitted values. Each type is a
concrete subclass of the abstract \code{ggseg_atlas} parent, and \code{wm_atlas}
descends from \code{subcortical_atlas} because a white-matter label map stores
and draws exactly what a subcortical atlas does. The abstract parent cannot
be instantiated, so an atlas of an unknown type is unrepresentable rather
than merely rejected.
}
\details{
\code{type} is a read-only property computed from the class, which is why there
is no way to leave the two disagreeing. Retyping an atlas therefore means
constructing a new one; see \code{\link[=set_atlas_type]{set_atlas_type()}}.
}
